<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sidney Shippard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Sidney Shippard was a British colonial official and judge in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He was administrator of British Bechuanaland from 1885 to 1895 and deputy commissioner for the Bechuanaland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>, and he supported the extension of British influence, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>'s interests, northwards towards Matabeleland.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sidney Godolphin Alexander Shippard was born on 29 May 1837, the eldest son of Captain William Shippard of the 29th Regiment, himself a son of Rear Admiral Alexander Shippard, and of Elizabeth Lydia Peters. He was educated at King's College School and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, took his degree in 1863 and was called to the bar at the Inner Temple in 1867. In 1864 he married Maria Stockenström, daughter of Sir Andries Stockenström; after her early death in 1870 he married Rosalind Ayshford Sanford of Nynehead Court, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Shippard was attorney-general of Griqualand West from 1873 to 1877 and then acting recorder of its High Court, but retired a year later after clashes with Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartle-frere">Henry Bartle Frere</a> and Lieutenant Governor Sir William Owen Lanyon. From 1880 to 1885 he was a judge of the Supreme Court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, and in 1884 and 1885 he sat as British commissioner on the Anglo-German commission on British claims at Lüderitz Bay.</p><p>He had discussed plans for a British advance into south central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, and he saw the German annexation of Damaraland and Namaqualand as a step towards a German belt across the continent. After the Warren expedition of 1885 he organised the new British possessions in Bechuanaland, as administrator, chief magistrate and president of the Land Commission. He kept up a friendly correspondence with the Matabele king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a>, and at the end of 1887 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> persuaded the high commissioner, Sir Hercules Robinson, to sanction a treaty binding Lobengula to cede no territory to any power but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He was made a Knight Commander of the Order of St Michael and St George in 1887.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shippard left Bechuanaland in 1895. In 1896 he played an unofficial part in the negotiations between Sir Hercules Robinson and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> reformers after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a>. He then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 29 March 1902. He left his own account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a> treaty in an article on Bechuanaland published in British Africa in 1899. His widow, Lady Shippard, died in 1935.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sidney Shippard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An administrator, Sir <b>Sidney Shippard</b>, governed the colony, which included the lands of the Tlhaping and Barolong.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-bechuanaland">British Bechuanaland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brussels metropolitan area, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1879" aria-label="Brussels metropolitan area on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sidney Shippard"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 5772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 5772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1879">What was here in 1879? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
